{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c354","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1a0bc-8f18-7890-9a44-183867e3d858"},"sequence":4,"recorded_at":1780531400000004,"record_type":"status","durability":"ephemeral","causation_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","payload_type":"turn.input.user","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"turn_input_user","command_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e"},"prompt":"Final focused review, English, no tools or edits, under 350 words. Earlier Grok and Muse approved replacing marked.lexer(text) with new marked.Lexer().blockTokens(normalizedText), caching normalized text and offsets, plus lastIndexOf instead of split for last fence line. Both reviewers said a fresh lexer isolates its queue. Local verification found this incomplete: const a=new marked.Lexer(), b=new marked.Lexer(); a.options===b.options, a.options.tokenizer===b.options.tokenizer, and a.options===marked.defaults are all true. The shared tokenizer has lexer backreference. Its last undrained queue can retain text globally. Final patch now uses:\nconst lexer = new marked.Lexer({ ...marked.defaults, tokenizer: undefined })\ntokens = lexer.blockTokens(normalizedText.slice(stablePrefixLength))\nlexer.inlineQueue.length = 0\nThe installed public constructor chooses options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer, then stores tokenizer.lexer=this. With independent options and undefined tokenizer this creates an invocation-owned tokenizer. On throw, existing whole-text fallback is retained; no shared root retains this local cycle. Production only consumes type/raw. No global marked.use/options usage exists in this repository. Added regression constructs other=new marked.Lexer(); other.blockTokens('Other **message**'); snapshots its inlineQueue, invokes the production builder, then requires other.options.tokenizer.lexer===other and its pending queue unchanged. All current focused tests pass. Re-evaluate the lifetime claim and identify any concrete correctness issue in this final change. The original CRLF normalization and block semantic parity checks remain. No WASM was adopted; unpublished sibling diff-wasm remains duplicate-only and is not consumed. This is not an end-to-end speed claim.\n"}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c356","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1a0bc-8f18-7890-9a44-183867e3d858"},"sequence":5,"recorded_at":1780531400000006,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","payload_type":"run.lifecycle.started","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_started","command_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e"},"prompt":"Final focused review, English, no tools or edits, under 350 words. Earlier Grok and Muse approved replacing marked.lexer(text) with new marked.Lexer().blockTokens(normalizedText), caching normalized text and offsets, plus lastIndexOf instead of split for last fence line. Both reviewers said a fresh lexer isolates its queue. Local verification found this incomplete: const a=new marked.Lexer(), b=new marked.Lexer(); a.options===b.options, a.options.tokenizer===b.options.tokenizer, and a.options===marked.defaults are all true. The shared tokenizer has lexer backreference. Its last undrained queue can retain text globally. Final patch now uses:\nconst lexer = new marked.Lexer({ ...marked.defaults, tokenizer: undefined })\ntokens = lexer.blockTokens(normalizedText.slice(stablePrefixLength))\nlexer.inlineQueue.length = 0\nThe installed public constructor chooses options.tokenizer || new Tokenizer, then stores tokenizer.lexer=this. With independent options and undefined tokenizer this creates an invocation-owned tokenizer. On throw, existing whole-text fallback is retained; no shared root retains this local cycle. Production only consumes type/raw. No global marked.use/options usage exists in this repository. Added regression constructs other=new marked.Lexer(); other.blockTokens('Other **message**'); snapshots its inlineQueue, invokes the production builder, then requires other.options.tokenizer.lexer===other and its pending queue unchanged. All current focused tests pass. Re-evaluate the lifetime claim and identify any concrete correctness issue in this final change. The original CRLF normalization and block semantic parity checks remain. No WASM was adopted; unpublished sibling diff-wasm remains duplicate-only and is not consumed. This is not an end-to-end speed claim.\n"}}
