{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c41a","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1a0bc-8f18-7890-9a44-183867e3d858"},"sequence":103,"recorded_at":1780531400000202,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","payload_type":"run.terminal.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_terminal","command_id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0bc-9738-7690-a4d7-130d9d5c015e"},"terminal":"completed","text":"**Verdict:** The lifetime claim is sound for the new lexer, with one fallback caveat. I find no concrete correctness defect in the final change under the stated repository conditions.\n\nThe spread creates invocation-owned top-level `options`, and explicitly passing `tokenizer: undefined` makes the constructor allocate a new `Tokenizer` instead of reusing `marked.defaults.tokenizer`. Setting `tokenizer.lexer = this` therefore creates only a local cycle:\n\n`lexer → options/tokenizer → lexer`, plus `inlineQueue` entries before clearing.\n\nJavaScript garbage collection handles unreachable cycles, so neither success nor a `blockTokens` throw leaves that cycle reachable from a shared root. Clearing `inlineQueue` additionally drops pending inline source strings promptly. Returned block tokens still retain their `raw` text while production holds them, which is expected.\n\nThe regression is meaningful: because the separately constructed `other` lexer uses the shared tokenizer, checking that `other.options.tokenizer.lexer === other` and that a snapshot of its pending queue is unchanged proves production did not rebind or drain the shared tokenizer.\n\nResidual caveats:\n\n- The options copy is shallow. Nested defaults objects remain shared. This is not a demonstrated bug in the installed implementation or current repo usage, but it is not complete deep isolation.\n- If the whole-text error fallback literally calls global `marked.lexer(text)`, that fallback can again leave its queue on the shared tokenizer until another global lex overwrites it. The precise claim should therefore be: the new local lexer retains nothing globally; the retained fallback has the old shared-retention behavior on throw paths.\n- Suffix parsing still depends on `stablePrefixLength` being a genuine block boundary; the retained parity checks are the right guard.\n\nNo issue with consuming only `type`/`raw` after discarding the inline queue.","reason":null}}
